</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used two keyword spotting datasets developed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. The Spiking Heidelberg Digits (SHD) consist of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\,420" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10 420</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\,420</annotation></semantics></math> spoken digits (0-9) in German and English, spoken by 12 speakers. The Spiking Speech Commands (SSC) consist of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="105\,829" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>105 829</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">105\,829</annotation></semantics></math> samples, from Google Speech Commands, featuring 35 words spoken by a larger speaker pool.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.13757v1/gpu_to_loihi.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/125;" width="476" height="125" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>We train models with GeNN and export them in the NetX format for inference on Loihi.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The audio data from both datasets was transformed into spikes across 700 input channels using an artificial cochlea model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spiking Neural Networks</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> extended EventProp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> to be suitable for more complex tasks, and implemented the extensions in mlGeNN. More recently, delay learning was derived and implemented in the framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
We trained both single-layer recurrent and 2-layer feedforward networks of Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) neurons and exponential synapses with and without delays. Output neurons are non-firing leaky integrators, and the classification decision is based on the maximum of the exponentially weighted voltage integrals of the output neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For SHD, we used 512 hidden neurons, while for SSC, we used 1024 hidden neurons. We conducted hyperparameter searches over timestep sizes (<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{\,}\mathrm{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{\,}\mathrm{-}</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\text{\,}\mathrm{ms}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">ms</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\text{\,}\mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math>), for various delay initialisation ranges from a uniform distribution and regularisation strengths, with the target firing rate of hidden neurons being set at <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14\text{\,}\mathrm{Hz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">Hz</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14\text{\,}\mathrm{Hz}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the lack of an explicit validation set for SHD, we conducted cross-validation on the training set. For SSC we chose our model based on results obtained on the validation set. We report our best-performing models for recurrent and feedforward networks, with and without delays.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T2" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Spiking Heidelberg Digits classification performance comparison.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S2.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
